+
+# resolvePointer semantics
+
+## Context
+Later features build on `resolvePointer`, and the library must follow the published standard.
+RFC 6901 leaves some behaviour to the caller: what "does not resolve" means.
+
+## Decision
+- Strict validation: a pointer is `""` or starts with `/`; `~` must be followed by `0` or `1`.
+  The URI fragment form (`#/a`) is not accepted; it is not a valid pointer string.
+- Array tokens must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*` (no leading zeros, signs or decimals) and be in range.
+  `-` always throws: it names a nonexistent element, so it does not resolve to a value.
+- Only own properties of objects resolve, so `/__proto__`, `/constructor` and `/length` never
+  reach inherited members. Primitives and `null` have no children.
+- Resolution errors are plain `Error`s; a stored `undefined`/`null` value is returned as is.
+
+## Consequences
+Safe against prototype lookups; callers needing the "append" `-` semantic (e.g. a future JSON Patch)
+must handle it themselves before resolving. Any pointer that is invalid anywhere throws, even if an
+earlier token would already fail to resolve.
